From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patryk Kowalczyk <papyszoo@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:15:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: the AX output is pumped by any idle core, so a long
 frame on core 1 no longer drains the queue

Cemu queues one 3 ms AX frame from its main core's idle loop. An 80 ms frame on that core (a loaded
warp-in) is 16-22 ms longer than the 58-64 ms of audio in flight, so the console's queue runs dry
and the arrival crackles.

Any idle emulated core's loop now asks for the same pump, at the same idleEventsUs spacing, while
the main core runs or sits in a host call. A non-blocking gate lets one caller into AXOut_update at
a time; the others leave the turn's frame to it, so the pacing, Cemu's one-frame-in-flight check and
the output queue's locking are as they were. The pacing arithmetic moves into the port's Pacer
unchanged (3 ms, 2.9 ms while the backend wants blocks, 1.7 ms minimum, 216 ms skip) and stays on
host time. bettervr.json's audioAnyCore (0) restores the main core alone.

The diagnostics for a warp: one [stall] line per frame over 34 ms with its split as far as the
counters give (waiting for the game, record and replay, the six GPU waits, the AX feed gap), the
[perf] line's AX gap per 10 s (frames, longest gap between frames and blocks, skips, which cores
fed them), and the AudioOut queue's lowest fill and longest grain wait every 10 s, unconditionally.
A drain the producer recovers from was silent before.

Built, untested on console.
